# ASIC Design Flow — Part 1: Specification

## Overview

This part introduces the ASIC design flow, ASIC versus FPGA implementation, full-custom and standard-cell design, specification, hardware/software partitioning, and the front-end and back-end design flow.

---

## ASIC

ASIC stands for Application-Specific Integrated Circuit.

An ASIC is an integrated circuit designed for a specific application or purpose.

ASICs are suitable for high-volume production because the initial development and fabrication cost can be distributed across a large number of chips.

### Basic idea

High initial cost
→ Large production volume
→ Cost distributed across many chips
→ Lower cost per chip

---

## FPGA

FPGA stands for Field-Programmable Gate Array.

An FPGA is programmable hardware that can be configured after manufacturing.

FPGAs are useful when flexibility is important or when the production volume does not justify manufacturing a dedicated ASIC.

---

## ASIC vs FPGA

| ASIC | FPGA |
|---|---|
| Application-specific | Programmable |
| Manufactured for a specific design | Configured after manufacturing |
| High initial development/fabrication cost | More flexible |
| Suitable for high-volume production | Useful for specialized/lower-volume applications |
| Can be highly optimized | Can be reconfigured |

The choice depends on factors such as production volume, performance, power, area, cost and flexibility.

---

## Full-Custom Design

Full-custom design allows the designer to customize the implementation at a low level, including transistor, cell and layout design.

It may be used when:

- A suitable standard-cell library is unavailable
- Highly customized implementation is required
- Specific optimization is required
- Custom analog or mixed-signal blocks are required

---

## Standard-Cell Design

A standard-cell based design uses an existing library of pre-designed and characterized cells.

Examples include:

- NAND
- NOR
- AND
- OR
- NOT
- Multiplexer
- Flip-flop

Instead of designing every cell from scratch, the design can use the available library cells.

---

## Full-Custom vs Standard-Cell

### Full-Custom

- Highly customized
- Greater control over implementation
- More design effort
- Can include transistor/cell/layout customization

### Standard-Cell

- Uses predefined library cells
- Less design effort than full-custom
- Commonly used for digital ASIC implementation
- Depends on availability of suitable libraries

