## Specification

Specification is the first stage of the ASIC design flow.

The specification describes what the system needs to do.

Customer requirements are identified and converted into system requirements and specifications.

### Basic flow

Customer Requirements
↓
Marketing
↓
Architect
↓
Specification

Marketing teams interact with customers and identify desired features and requirements.

Architects use these requirements to define the system specification.

---

## Hardware/Software Partitioning

An electronic system can be divided into:

Hardware + Software

### Hardware implementation options

- ASIC
- FPGA
- PLD
- Standard chip

The architect determines which functionality should be implemented in hardware and which should be handled by software.

---

## SoC

SoC stands for System-on-Chip.

An SoC integrates multiple system functions and blocks onto a single chip.

Typical SoC components can include:

- Processor
- Memory
- I/O
- Peripherals
- Other IP blocks

Integrating multiple functions into an SoC can reduce the number of separate chips required in a system.

---

## IP

IP stands for Intellectual Property.

An IP block is a reusable hardware design block that can be integrated into a larger chip or SoC.

Examples:

- Processor core
- Memory controller
- USB controller
- UART
- PCIe controller

---

## Physical Design

Physical design converts the logical design into a physical layout.

Major stages include:

### Floorplanning

Planning the physical organization of the chip and major blocks.

### Macro Placement

Placing large blocks or macros in appropriate physical locations.

### Clock Tree

Implementing the clock distribution network.

### Routing

Creating physical interconnections between cells and blocks.

### STA

STA stands for Static Timing Analysis.

STA is used to analyze timing behavior and timing constraints in the design.

### Layout

Represents the physical implementation of the circuit on silicon.

### DRC

DRC stands for Design Rule Checking.

DRC checks whether the physical layout satisfies the manufacturing design rules.

### LVS

LVS stands for Layout Versus Schematic.

LVS checks whether the layout connectivity matches the intended circuit.

### GDSII

GDSII stands for Graphic Design System II.

GDSII represents the physical layout data used in the semiconductor manufacturing flow.

